# Phase 18 consumer stats manager API/sharing closure and current-revision P18-V02 refresh — 2026-10-21T17:14:04Z

Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`
Code revision: `0a927661` (`fix(consumer): share stats manager by runtime`)
Pinned Java source: `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.
Fixture SHA-256: `880825d176dae84dd0f9fdcb5354261a3cc6b160350e222722c4c8978cb234fd`.

## Source audit and change

- Java `DefaultMQPushConsumerImpl.getConsumerStatsManager()` returns the
  `MQClientInstance` manager. Multiple Push implementations sharing one
  factory share that manager; distinct `MQClientInstance`s own distinct
  managers, even with the same client ID.
- Moved the public Rust `ConsumerStatsManager` into Core runtime ownership and
  re-exported it through the Consumer and facade crates. Push/POP metric
  recording and Running Info use the same runtime manager. Getter behavior
  retains Java's unavailable state before factory binding and after nulling the
  implementation factory.
- Exposed the Java lifecycle, metric-update and status methods. The invoked
  Java fixture inventories the public constructor and all eight public
  methods, and checks shared factory identity.
- Compared `ConsumerStatsManager`, `StatsItemSet`, `StatsItem` and `RTStatsItem`
  with the pinned source. Rust uses Java's `topic@group` key construction and
  integer narrowing; it keeps the 10-second/10-minute sampling windows and
  does not automatically expire client stats entries. The consumer-side
  `cleanResource` call sites are broker stats only.
- Added regressions for same-runtime sharing, distinct-runtime isolation,
  metric group partitioning, Java key collisions, and each mapped public
  manager method. Updated the Phase 6, 10 and 17 compatibility records.

